form closer to sub-Gaussian. Under low-precision weight-only quantization, per-weight error scales with the magnitude of quantized entries; reducing outlier mass therefore lowers quantization error, most where bits are scarce.\n\nH2 (testable claim): Quantized-quality loss (\u0394PPL vs. the fp16 anchor) is strictly smaller with the weight-side Hadamard rotation than without it, for BOTH RTN and GPTQ, at 2, 3, and 4 bits; the absolute gain increases as bit width decreases (2-bit > 3-bit > 4-bit), and RTN benefits more than GPTQ (GPTQ already re-distributes error via Hessian compensation, so it has less headroom).\n\nFixed task requirements being operationalized: weight-only quantization only (mapping to setup_5/setup_7 \u2014 weights quantized, activations stay fp16) at 2, 3, 4 bits (setup_11), with RTN (setup_16) and GPTQ (setup_17) as the two control baselines. QuaRot enters strictly through its offline weight-side Hadamard rotations (assumption A5 below), since QuaRot is activation-oriented.\n\nDesign: 2 (rotation: absent / Hadamard-present) \u00d7 2 (quantizer: RTN / GPTQ) \u00d7 3 (bits: 2/3/4) = 12 primary cells on a concrete named model (OPT-125M), plus ablation cells and fp16 references; 3 seeds per cell, with per-bit condition tables as the result format.\n\n## 2. Precise operationalization\n\n### 2.1 Factorial cells\n| cell | rotation | quantizer | bits |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| C1 | absent | RTN | 2 |\n| C2 | absent | RTN | 3 |\n| C3 | absent | RTN | 4 |\n| C4 | absent | GPTQ | 2 |\n| C5 | absent | GPTQ | 3 |\n| C6 | absent | GPTQ | 4 |\n| C7 | Hadamard | RTN | 2 |\n| C8 | Hadamard | RTN | 3 |\n| C9 | Hadamard | RTN | 4 |\n| C10 | Hadamard | GPTQ | 2 |\n| C11 | Hadamard | GPTQ | 3 |\n| C12 | Hadamard | GPTQ | 4 |\nAblation cells (attribute the mechanism): A1 per-matrix Hadamard only (each Linear rotated column-wise, no block coordination); A2 generic random orthogonal matrix (Haar-uniform, from QR of a Gaussian) replacing Hadamard at all fusion points (tests structure vs. generic orthogonality). Every cell is run with seeds {0,1,2}.\nReference anchors (not 13th/14th cells): fp16 original model (R_orig) and fp16 fused-rotation model (R_fused) \u2014 must agree to ~1e-3 (validity check \u00a73).\n\n### 2.2 Hadamard construction (pinned, reproducible)\n- Base tiles: H2 = (1/\u221a2)\u00b7[[1,1],[1,\u22121]]; higher powers by Kronecker recursion H_{2k} = H2 \u2297 H_k, normalized by 1/\u221ak (H_k\u00b7H_k^T = I). QuaRot-style tiles H4, H8, H16 available via this recursion.\n- Application: block-diagonal \u2014 for a Linear with input width d, apply H over contiguous power-of-2 chunks of d. OPT-125M dims are all power-of-2 multiples, so no padding needed: d=768 \u2192 three H_256 blocks; d=3072 \u2192 twelve H_256 blocks; d=64 \u2192 one H_64. Default chunk = 256 for attention/MLP projections, H_64 for head-sized (64) projections. This \"block-diagonal Hadamard\" is exactly the QuaRot construction and keeps the transform a sparse Kronecker product (no dense 768\u00d7768 rotation).\n- Implementation is pinned (code-level): a single `hadamard(k)` function (recursion above) + `apply_rotation(weight, side, chunk)` used by all cells \u2192 identical matrices across seeds/runs.\n\n### 2.3 Rotation-fusion recipe for OPT-125M (weight-only, function-preserving)\nOPT-125M (HF) architecture used: hidden 768, 12 layers, 12 heads (head_dim 64), MLP 768\u21923072\u2192768, learned position embeddings, No RoPE. Each block: LN1 \u2192 self-attn(Q,K,V,O) \u2192 residual \u2192 LN2 \u2192 fc1(768\u21923072, gelu) \u2192 fc2(3072\u2192768) \u2192 residual. The fused-rotation recipe that leaves the fp16 function EXACT:\n- Embedding: E' = E\u00b7H_emb (right-multiply), so the first block's residual stream is in the rotated basis. H_emb = block-diagonal H_256 over width 768.\n- Per block with input-side rotation H1 (after LN1) and H3 (after LN2), and intermediate H2 for V:\n  - Q, K: Wq' = Wq\u00b7H1 ; Wk' = Wk\u00b7H1 (right-multiply; input-side rotation; dot-product invariance of the attention softmax keeps outputs identical).\n  - V: Wv' = Wv\u00b7H2 (right-multiply).\n  - O: Wo' = H2\u00b7Wo (left-multiply; maps the H2-rotated attention output back to the base residual basis before the residual add).\n  - fc1: Wfc1' = Wfc1\u00b7H3 (right-multiply; input-side).\n  - fc2: Wfc2' = H3\u00b7Wfc2 (left-multiply; maps the H3-rotated mlp output back to base basis before residual add).\n  - LayerNorms: unchanged \u2014 LN is row-wise and orthogonal-rotation-invariant (LN(H x) = LN(x)), which is what licenses placing R1/R3 right after LN1/LN2 with no extra fusion into the preceding O/fc2 in fp.\n  - Head/output: lm_head remains fp16, unrotated (standard for weight-only); the final LN2 is rotation-invariant so the output head sees the original basis.\n- Why this is EXACT in fp16: (i) H\u00b7H^T = I, (ii) LN orthogonal-invariance, (iii) attention dot-product invariance under a common orthogonal rotation of Q and K, (iv) OPT has no RoPE, so no commutation subtlety (for a LLaMA variant, QuaRot's RoPE-commuting Hadamard would be required \u2014 flagged as an uncertainty/variant, not used in the primary protocol).\n\n### 2.4 Quantization procedures per cell\n- RTN: per-channel symmetric round-to-nearest with scale = max|w|/(2^{bits\u22121}\u22121), zero-point 0 (symmetric, sym=True), group_size=128, no calibration, no fine-tuning. Applied to W (controls) or the rotated W' (Hadamard cells).\n- GPTQ: one-shot layer-wise Hessian-based weight quantization with calibration: 128 sequences \u00d7 2048 tokens from C4 train (fixed ordering, see \u00a76); group_size=128, sym=True, block_size=128, dampening=1e-2, act_order=False, greedy Hessian updates in blocks.\n- GPTQ-on-rotated-weights (Hadamard cells): the quantizer must see the SAME effective layer that inference will use. Recipe: propagate calibration activations through the fused-rotation fp16 model so each layer's inputs are in the rotated basis (they are, by construc